#     "1. A Closure includes any change of location considered by the U.S. Department of Education to be a closure of the existing Institution; and 2. In general, the Commission considers a Teach-Out Agreement to be practicable unless the Institution submitting a Teach-Out Plan can demonstrate that there are no Institutions within a 50-mile radius that (i) meet the requirements of Checklist Items 7 and 8 of this Policy and (ii) are willing to enter into a Teach-Out Agreement meeting the requirements of Checklist Items 4 and 5 of this Policy.",
#     "All accredited and candidate institutions must submit a Teach-Out Plan, including a draft Teach-Out Agreement if practicable, with each application for accreditation, renewal, and annual report submission, as required by NACCAS policy. In the event of a Teach-Out Event—such as a program or institutional closure—a Teach-Out Plan must be submitted within 15 days of the event if unplanned, or 30 days prior if planned. The plan must include a draft or executed Teach-Out Agreement and, when applicable, a list of accredited schools within a 50-mile radius that meet specific checklist requirements (Items 7 and 8). A Teach-Out Agreement is considered practicable unless the institution can prove there are no qualifying institutions within this radius. Importantly, issuing pro rata refunds to students does not replace the requirement to submit a Teach-Out Plan and Agreement. When a Teach-Out Agreement involves a program accredited by another recognized accrediting agency, NACCAS will notify that agency upon approval of the agreement.",
#     "A authorized leave of absence (LOA) is a temporary interruption in a student?s program of study. LOA refers to the specific time period during a program when a student is not in attendance. A LOA is not required if a student is not in attendance only for an institutionally scheduled break. However, a scheduled break may occur during a LOA. A LOA must meet certain conditions to be counted as a temporary interruption in a student?s education instead of being counted as a withdrawal requiring an institution to perform a refund calculation. If an institution elects to offer LOAs, it must have a written Leave of Absence Policy that complies with this NACCAS Leave of Absence Policy and Checklist. Additionally, approval of a student?s LOA must be in accordance with the institution?s Leave of Absence Policy. A LOA may only be granted if there is a reasonable expectation that the student will return from the LOA. This checklist has been prepared as a guide to assist schools in interpreting the requirements of this Policy and to serve as a guide in preparing a Leave of Absence Policy. It is not necessary for the various elements to be stated in any special phraseology or listed in any particular sequence, as long as the overall policy conveys the required elements in a way that can be easily understood.",
#     "If an institution chooses to offer Leaves of Absence (LOAs), its Leave of Absence Policy must meet specific requirements and be submitted to the Commission along with a completed checklist each time the policy is provided, including as a required exhibit in the Institutional Self-Study (ISS). The policy must be clearly written and titled as a Leave of Absence Policy. It must state the acceptable reasons for granting a LOA and require students to follow institutional procedures when requesting one. The policy must require that students submit LOA requests in advance unless unforeseen circumstances prevent it, and such requests must be in writing, include the student’s reason and signature. If the LOA request is submitted after the LOA begins due to unforeseen events, the institution must document the reason, obtain the request later, and use the first day of the student’s absence as the start date. It must also state that students will not incur additional charges due to a LOA and that LOAs may not exceed a combined total of 180 days within a 12-month period. Furthermore, a student on an approved LOA is not considered withdrawn, so no refund calculation is required at that time. The policy must specify that the student’s contract period will be extended by the length of the LOA, and such changes must be confirmed either by initialing the updated enrollment agreement or signing an addendum. If a student takes an unapproved LOA or fails to return after an approved LOA ends, they are considered withdrawn. For refund calculations, if the institution takes attendance, the last date of attendance is used as the withdrawal date; if not, the withdrawal date is the day the LOA began. Additionally, the revised contract end date due to a LOA must not exceed a 15-day limit.",
#     "A student who is beyond the age of compulsory education, lacks a high school diploma or its equivalent, and has the ability to benefit from the education or training offered at an institution.",
#     "A school applying for NACCAS accreditation or in candidacy status cannot claim to be 'accredited by NACCAS' or imply accreditation in any way, as this would be misleading. However, if the school has been granted 'candidate status,' it may use the phrase 'candidate for accreditation' in its advertising. Schools that have never been accredited, have voluntarily relinquished accreditation, or have had accreditation withdrawn by NACCAS cannot advertise as accredited by NACCAS.",
#     "Yes, the use of 'Global Academy' on exterior signage and 'GACM' in social media advertising is permissible under the NACCAS policy, provided certain conditions are met. The policy allows the use of a shortened version of the school name (e.g., 'Global Academy' instead of 'Global Academy of Cosmetology and Massage') in instances where the school is clearly identifiable as the same institution. Similarly, abbreviations like 'GACM' are allowed in advertising, as the policy permits replacing words with numerical abbreviations or symbols (e.g., ampersands, Roman numerals) in promotional materials. However, the school must have listed any alternate names in its accreditation application, and the approved school name must be used on the institution’s homepage, though it is not required in the Social Media URL.",
#     "The school may advertise the 'Advanced Esthetics' pilot program, but it must adhere to the following requirements per the NACCAS policy: The school can advertise a maximum of one pilot program or course per year that does not yet have NACCAS approval to determine market interest. All advertising for the pilot program must clearly indicate that it is not yet accredited by NACCAS. This ensures transparency and prevents misleading prospective students about the program’s accreditation status.",
#     "No, a school named 'Elite Beauty Institute, LLC' cannot use 'LLC' in its advertising materials. The NACCAS policy prohibits the use of words, phrases, or abbreviations designating a corporate entity, such as 'Limited Liability Company' or 'LLC,' in the school’s name for advertising purposes. The school must advertise as 'Elite Beauty Institute' to comply with the policy.",
#     "To comply with NACCAS advertising standards, the school must: Ensure the claim ('90% of our graduates are employed within six months of graduation') is supported by current data sufficient to prove its truthfulness. Maintain the supporting data for a minimum of three years. Make the supporting data available for review by the Commission and the general public. Ensure the claim is accurate and not misleading, deceptive, fraudulent, or false, as required for all advertising directed at prospective students. Failure to meet these requirements would violate the policy’s standards for quantitative claims in advertising.",
#     "Yes, the transcript showing completion of a two-year associate’s degree program satisfies the educational requirements for admission, as the NACCAS policy recognizes proof of attainment of an associate’s degree or higher (via a college transcript showing college completion or degree) as an equivalent to a high school diploma. The school must maintain a copy of the college transcript in the student’s file to document this qualification.",
#     "The homeschooled student must provide the secondary school completion credential issued by the state to be eligible for enrollment, as the policy requires this credential if available in the state where the student was homeschooled. Additionally, the school must have a policy that explicitly states it accepts homeschooled students, and the student’s secondary school education must have been in a homeschool that state law treats as a home or private school.",
#     "No, a student who is still enrolled in high school cannot enroll in an associate’s degree program without a high school diploma. The NACCAS policy requires that for students enrolling in a program leading to an associate’s degree in lieu of completing high school, the student must no longer be enrolled in high school, must provide a high school transcript indicating they excelled in high school, and must satisfy the school’s written policy for admitting such students prior to the first day of class.",
#     "For ability-to-benefit (ATB) students, the school must maintain: Documentation of the results of each student’s test, verifying a passing score. Information on the third-party test administrator, including their name, address, and any identifier assigned by the test publisher or state. Alternatively, the policy allows ATB students to demonstrate their ability to benefit by satisfactorily completing 6 credit hours or 225 clock hours after enrollment, instead of taking a test prior to enrollment.",
#     "The school must ensure the student meets the admission requirements set out in the training agreement and any applicable state licensing or certification regulations. The student is not considered an enrollee of the NACCAS-accredited institution and is not counted in the NACCAS Annual Report. The school is not required to have a separate contract with the student but must maintain a copy of the training agreement along with a list of students enrolled through it. The agreement must specify the school’s obligations, such as reporting attendance and grades",
#     "The school must retain the student’s ATB-related records until at least 2029, as the policy requires records to be kept for a minimum of six years following the student’s completion of the program (2023 + 6 = 2029). The required records include documentation of the student’s satisfactory completion of 225 clock hours, counseling records, and records pertaining to the student’s enrollment.",
#     "No, this does not comply with the NACCAS Ability-to-Benefit Policy. For a 500-hour program, an ATB student must complete a nationally recognized, standardized, or industry-developed test prior to admission to demonstrate their ability to benefit, as the program is less than 600 hours and the post-enrollment option (6 credit hours or 225 clock hours) is not applicable. Admitting the student without meeting either requirement violates the policy.",
#     "No, this does not comply with the NACCAS Enrollment Agreement Requirements policy. The policy prohibits collecting any payments from an applicant, other than a non-refundable application fee not exceeding $100, before entering into an enrollment agreement. The $200 registration fee exceeds the allowed application fee and was collected prematurely, violating the policy.",
#     "A copy of the NACCAS Enrollment Agreement Checklist must accompany the enrollment agreement submitted to the Commission. The checklist serves as a guide to ensure the agreement contains all required elements per NACCAS standards and assists schools in interpreting accreditation standards when preparing contracts.",
#     "This does not comply with the NACCAS Policy on Occupational Associate Degree Programs for three reasons: While letters of recommendation from a former employer may justify an exception to the associate degree requirement, the policy requires that such documentation (e.g., letters) be available for review. Failing to retain the letters violates this requirement. The syllabus must include the date it was last reviewed as part of the minimum requirements. Omitting this date violates the syllabus requirements. The externship agreement must include evaluation criteria, along with specific learning objectives and course requirements. Lacking evaluation criteria violates the externship guidelines.",
#     "The minimum requirements for a course syllabus include: Title and course description. Course number. Course prerequisites and/or requisites. Instructional contact hours/credits. Learning objectives. Instructional materials and references. Topical outline of the course. Instructional methods. Out-of-class learning activities and assignments. Assessment criteria. Method of evaluating students. The date the syllabus was last reviewed.",
#     "To calculate the semester credit hours: Lecture: 300 clock hours ÷ 15 clock hours per semester credit hour = 20 semester credit hours. Lab: 300 clock hours ÷ 30 clock hours per semester credit hour = 10 semester credit hours. Clinical: 300 clock hours ÷ 45 clock hours per semester credit hour = 6.67 semester credit hours (rounded to 7, as partial credits are typically rounded up or per institutional policy). Total: 20 + 10 + 7 = 37 semester credit hours.",
#     "The disaster recovery plan must include: Financial Information: A written financial plan projecting the revenues, expenditures, and cash flow of the school at its location, including all startup costs (e.g., building, equipment, staffing) and projected enrollments and clinic revenues. Enrollment Information: Enrollment projections, including: Enrollment prior to the disaster. Returning students after the disaster. Projected enrollment at the current location",
#     "To comply with the NACCAS Policy on Disasters, the institution must: Notify NACCAS immediately of the cessation of teaching for days not listed in the catalog due to the hurricane damage. If the institution is unable to resume full compliance at its approved location within 30 calendar days, it must submit a written plan and timeline within 30 calendar days, detailing how it plans to resume operations. The notification must be sent to Darin M. Wallace, Esq., Executive Director 3015 Colvin Street, Alexandria, VA 22314 Phone: (703) 600-7600, Fax: (703) 379-2200 Email: dwallace@naccas.org",
#     "If the institution charges the student for extra instruction prior to the completion date on the enrollment agreement, it must secure an acknowledgment of credit to the student’s account for the payment. This ensures transparency and accountability for the charges applied before program completion",
#     "The institution can charge the student for the additional 30 hours of instruction because: The student has exhausted all allowed absences within the contract period. The enrollment agreement clearly states the amount charged per unit of instruction ($10 per clock hour). The institution must ensure: The tuition and fees information, including the $10 per clock hour charge, was provided to the student upon enrollment. If the student terminates before completing the contract, all monies received for the extra instruction (30 hours × $10 = $300) are refunded. If the institution charges the fee prior to the completion date on the enrollment agreement, it secures an acknowledgment of credit to the student’s account.",
#     "According to the NACCAS Minimum Tuition Adjustment Schedule, a student who completes 10% of the program (60 hours of a 600-hour program) owes 40% of the total tuition to the school. Any monies due to the student must be refunded within 45 calendar days of the determination of the official or unofficial withdrawal.",
#     "In the absence of state-mandated content, the Electrology program curriculum must include: Sciences: Infection control Electricity Anatomy and physiology Disease/Disorders (Hair, skin) Electrolysis: Use of Electrical Currents Modalities: Thermolysis, Galvanic, Blend Career and employment information: Professional ethics Communication skills Communication methods Income reporting Licensing or certification requirements State laws and regulations Business basics Record keeping The institution must also develop course outlines and use lesson plans that correlate to these components.",
#     "For a Hairdressing-only program in a state with no mandated curriculum content, the institution can exclude the following Cosmetology curriculum components: Principles and techniques for the skin (Skin care treatments, Makeup, Hair removal, Contraindications). Principles and techniques for the nails (Manicure, Pedicure, Artificial nails). The institution must still include: Sciences: Infection Control Chemistry Electricity Anatomy and physiology Structure/Disease/Disorders (Hair, Skin, Nails) Principles and techniques of Hair: Hair styling Hair shaping Hair coloring Chemical texture services Scalp and hair treatments Shampooing Career and employment information: Professional ethics Communication skills Compensation methods Income reporting Licensing or certification requirements State laws and regulations Business basics Record keeping",
#     "In the absence of state-mandated content, a Cosmetology program must include the following curriculum components: Sciences: Infection Control Chemistry Electricity Anatomy and physiology Structure/Disease/Disorders (Hair, Skin, Nails) Principles and techniques of Hair: Hair styling Hair shaping Hair coloring Chemical texture services Scalp and hair treatments Shampooing Principles and techniques for the skin (not required for Hairdressing-only programs): Skin care treatments Makeup Hair removal Contraindications Principles and techniques for the nails (not required for Hairdressing-only programs): Manicure Pedicure Artificial nails Career and employment information: Professional ethics Communication skills Compensation methods Income reporting Licensing or certification requirements State laws and regulations Business basics Record keeping",
#     "The institution must ensure that its distance education policy validates substantive and regular interaction as follows: Substantive Interaction: Engage students in teaching, learning, and assessment, including at least two of the following: Providing direct instruction. Assessing or providing feedback on student’s distance education coursework. Providing information or responding to questions about coursework content. Facilitating a group discussion regarding coursework content. Regular Interaction: Provide opportunities for substantive interactions with students on a scheduled basis. Monitor students’ academic engagement and ensure the instructor is responsible for substantive interaction.",
#     "To comply with the NACCAS Externship Requirements Policy: The institution must select the student based on written criteria and ensure the student meets these criteria. The student must have taken and passed a comprehensive written and practical examination establishing their qualification to participate in the externship course.",
#     "This does not fully comply with the NACCAS Internal Grievance Procedure Policy for two reasons: The policy requires students to be informed of the grievance policy at the beginning of the course or program. Failing to communicate the policy to the student at the program’s start violates this requirement. While the policy requires complaint forms to be made available to students, providing them only upon request may not meet the standard of being readily accessible, depending on how easily students can obtain them. To address the situation, the institution should: Immediately inform the student of the grievance policy and provide clear instructions on the steps to file a formal grievance, including access to the complaint form. Ensure the complaint form is provided promptly to the student. Revise its practices to ensure all future students are informed of the grievance policy at the beginning of their program and that complaint forms are readily available (e.g., through a student handbook, website, or physical copies in an accessible location). Retain records of the student’s complaint and its resolution for review by the NACCAS on-site visit team.",
#     "The institution must take the following steps: Notify the student that they are not meeting Satisfactory Academic Progress and that this impacts their eligibility for Title IV funds, as required by the policy. Place the student on SAP warning (if the institution uses this status) or proceed to probation if the student appeals. If the student appeals, evaluate the appeal based on the institution’s policy, which must include: How the student may re-establish eligibility for financial aid. Reasons for which a student may appeal, such as death of a relative, injury or illness of the student, or other allowable special circumstances. Documentation the student must submit explaining why they failed to make SAP and what has changed to allow achievement of SAP at the next evaluation. How the results of the appeal are documented in the student’s file. If the appeal is approved, place the student on probation if: The institution determines SAP standards can be met by the end of the subsequent evaluation period, or An academic plan is developed to ensure the student meets SAP requirements by a specific point within the maximum time frame. If the student does not prevail on appeal or fails to meet SAP during probation, they are no longer eligible for Title IV funds. The SAP policy must describe the appeal process, including the above elements, to ensure compliance.",
#     "The institution must take the following steps: Notify the student that they are not meeting Satisfactory Academic Progress, as required by the policy. Place the student on SAP warning (if the institution uses this status) or proceed to probation if the student appeals. If the student appeals, evaluate the appeal based on the institution’s policy, which must include: How the student may re-establish eligibility for financial aid (if applicable). Reasons for which a student may appeal, such as death of a relative, injury or illness of the student, or other allowable special circumstances. Documentation the student must submit explaining why they failed to make SAP and what has changed to allow achievement of SAP at the next evaluation. How the results of the appeal are documented in the student’s file. If the appeal is approved, place the student on probation if: The institution determines SAP standards can be met by the end of the subsequent evaluation period, or An academic plan is developed to ensure the student meets SAP requirements by a specific point within the maximum time frame. If the student does not prevail on appeal or fails to meet SAP during probation, they are no longer eligible for Title IV funds (if applicable). The SAP policy must describe the appeal process, including the above elements, to ensure compliance."
